{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-127072" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-1,0),(2,0),(2,2),$ and $(-1,2),$ traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$F(x,y)={x}^{2}y\\,i+5xy\\,j$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127072" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/265d27ad87a7f114bd2a32a5f48cbfedb13192abf48f9b759c4ed635e71ff144.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=5xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+5x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x(2y+5)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127072" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f1c4f276d3f3f1f755a0d39202b7b6efa6ee744e55963187e190c2f2a6a4a368"} {"question" "q-127072" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127072" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}+5y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}-5y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x(2y+5)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}x(2y+5)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}x(2y-5)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127072" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x(2y+5)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "According to the flux form of Green's theorem, the flux of the vector field \n$F(x,y)=⟨y+2{x}^{2}y,\\,{x}^{2}-x{y}^{2}⟩$\nacross the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(0,0),(2,0),(2,1),$ and  $(0,1)$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise, $n$ is the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C,$ and $D$ is the region bounded by $C.$ Calculate the function $f(x,y).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/98d7da0c7cd1e1f1006c1db74ae1a1e1f198d9066df6585745ee36548f0b8d96.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=y+2{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}-x{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=4xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the flux form of Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4xy+(-2xy))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=2xy.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f1c4f276d3f3f1f755a0d39202b7b6efa6ee744e55963187e190c2f2a6a4a368"} {"question" "q-127206" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127206" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2xy"] ["math" "2xy"] ["math" "6xy"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127206" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2xy"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-1,0),(1,0),(1,2),$ and $(-1,2),$ traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$F(x,y)=3x{y}^{3}\\,i+6x{y}^{4}\\,j$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/fa98f3db31860a463d0a551d5297895e61a6c041d852c04de04c44a194510961.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=3x{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=6x{y}^{4}.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=3{y}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =24x{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{y}^{3}+24x{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{3}(1+8x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f1c4f276d3f3f1f755a0d39202b7b6efa6ee744e55963187e190c2f2a6a4a368"} {"question" "q-127229" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127229" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(6{y}^{2}+8x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{3}(1+8x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{3}(1+8x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(6{y}^{2}+8x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}6{y}^{2}(1+8x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127229" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{3}(1+8x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127228" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(0,0),(3,0),(3,2),$ and $(0,2),$  traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$F(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,i+12xy\\,j$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127228" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/23066b23aebf332921c22a0439a414ade8dffd2a270641d186da0ed0e9488821.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=12xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =12x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x{y}^{2}+12x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2x({y}^{2}+6)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127228" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f1c4f276d3f3f1f755a0d39202b7b6efa6ee744e55963187e190c2f2a6a4a368"} {"question" "q-127228" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127228" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(2{y}^{2}+6)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{2}+12y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}2{y}^{2}(x+6)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2{x}^{2}+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2x({y}^{2}+6)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127228" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2x({y}^{2}+6)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127231" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-3,-1),(-1,-1),(-1,1),$ and $(-3,1),$ traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$F(x,y)=(y{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}x)\\,i+(-{y}^{2}x+{y}^{4})\\,j$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127231" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/5497f23e51d1e745fef1cf39d6c1a8ada22b25e8c9c7b87f5d5338e85a941739.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=y{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}x$ and $Q(x,y)=-{y}^{2}x+{y}^{4}.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2yx+{y}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2yx+4{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-3\\le x\\le -1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2yx+{y}^{3})+(-2yx+4{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{-1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}5{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127231" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f1c4f276d3f3f1f755a0d39202b7b6efa6ee744e55963187e190c2f2a6a4a368"} {"question" "q-127231" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127231" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{-1}{\\int }_{-3}^{1}(5{x}^{3}+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{-1}{\\int }_{-3}^{1}5{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}5{y}^{3}(x-5)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}5{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{3}(2xy+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127231" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}5{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127322" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨\\sqrt[3]{y}+x,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(0,0),(2,0),(2,1),$ and $(0,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127322" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/ea6c25e40b6683d9ff4a4884dbbda708fd79dbd79d0b50730f9ad6e165d2eb58.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{y}+x$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-6{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-6{x}^{2}y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(1-6{x}^{2}y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-2{x}^{3}y)\\,{|}_{0}^{2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[(2-2(2)^{3}y)-0]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-16y)\\,dy \\\\  & =[2y-8{y}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(2(1)-8(1)^{2})-0 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127322" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f1c4f276d3f3f1f755a0d39202b7b6efa6ee744e55963187e190c2f2a6a4a368"} {"question" "q-127322" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127322" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "-6"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127322" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-6"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127308" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-3{x}^{3},\\,x{e}^{x}-4x{y}^{3}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle $-1\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le 1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127308" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/2696cdadd3cb020cfbe343b9c372893ac18b63f3548ff71003b7328b19de39d5.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-3{x}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=x{e}^{x}-4x{y}^{3}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-9{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-12x{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-9{x}^{2}-12x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-9{x}^{2}-12x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[-3{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}]_{-1}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[(-3(1)^{3}-6(1)^{2}{y}^{2})-(-3(-1)^{3}-6(-1)^{2}{y}^{2})]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-6)\\,dy \\\\  & =-6y\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127308" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f1c4f276d3f3f1f755a0d39202b7b6efa6ee744e55963187e190c2f2a6a4a368"} {"question" "q-127308" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127308" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-12"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "-5"] ["math" "-6"] ["math" "e-6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127308" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-6"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127324" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)-y{x}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+2xy⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square with vertices $(0,-1),(1,-1),(1,0),$ and $(0,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127324" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/3497b3bbc834879026235f97468f5feb26e91a2b73a4de3da87597bf422ecd33.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)-y{x}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+2xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2xy+2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x-2xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{2}-{x}^{2}y)\\,{|}_{0}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[(1-y)-0]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(1-y)\\,dy \\\\  & =[y-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-((-1)-\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127324" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f1c4f276d3f3f1f755a0d39202b7b6efa6ee744e55963187e190c2f2a6a4a368"} {"question" "q-127324" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127324" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127324" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127303" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+2x,\\,{x}^{5}-3y⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square $-1\\le x\\le 1,-1\\le y\\le 1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127303" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/35e378bca35f4b0822c713556065415d59b5c566ec8fcc8e3124256aac7b7f14.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+2x$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{5}-3y.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2-3)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}x\\,{|}_{-1}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1-(-1))\\,dy \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & =-2y\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127303" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f1c4f276d3f3f1f755a0d39202b7b6efa6ee744e55963187e190c2f2a6a4a368"} {"question" "q-127303" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127303" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-4"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127303" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-126820" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨{e}^{y}+2{x}^{2}y,\\,{e}^{x}+2x{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(-1,0),(2,0),(2,1),$ and $(-1,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-126820" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/f6437eddec7d44a10c7fb2b3461a09844ca06e5131b69503f51432eb87fedd31.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{y}+2{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{x}+2x{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=4xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4xy+4xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}8xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4{x}^{2}y\\,{|}_{-1}^{2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4(2)^{2}y-4(-1)^{2}y)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(16y-4y)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}12y\\,dy \\\\  & =6{y}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-126820" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f1c4f276d3f3f1f755a0d39202b7b6efa6ee744e55963187e190c2f2a6a4a368"} {"question" "q-126820" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-126820" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-126820" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127323" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨6{x}^{2}+2y,\\,-10y-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the triangle with vertices $(0,0),(2,1),$ and $(-2,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127323" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/3ad0a876fe801980a2c3724a639fc6c379db750635c0278a10a5094e2d64c34a.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=6{x}^{2}+2y$ and $Q(x,y)=-10y-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=12x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-2y\\le x\\le 2y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(12x-10)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-2y}^{2y}(12x-10)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[6{x}^{2}-10x]_{-2y}^{2y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(24{y}^{2}-20y)-(24{y}^{2}+20y)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-40y)\\,dy \\\\  & =-40{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-40(\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127323" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f1c4f276d3f3f1f755a0d39202b7b6efa6ee744e55963187e190c2f2a6a4a368"} {"question" "q-127323" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127323" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-20"] ["math" "-25"] ["math" "-\\frac{15}{2}"] ["math" "10"] ["math" "15"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127323" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-20"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127316" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨x-\\sqrt{y},\\,y-\\sqrt{x}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and the line $y=0$ for $0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127316" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/c4dd4d03bdfcf6c3d597e2fdef8681e4f3316b3f276cb9967933b61b0dc90976.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x-\\sqrt{y}$ and $Q(x,y)=y-\\sqrt{x}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}y\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,dx \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =2(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2(0-(-1)) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127316" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f1c4f276d3f3f1f755a0d39202b7b6efa6ee744e55963187e190c2f2a6a4a368"} {"question" "q-127316" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127316" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "3\\pi "] ["math" "5"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127316" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127325" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨xy-\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1},\\,{x}^{3}+y⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the triangle with vertices $(0,0),(1,2),$ and $(-1,2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127325" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/006d80a7dbe4d03b78cc5fb1a642405e3fdfdd69ec9d80014ee8e0355ad1ae73.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=xy-\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{3}+y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{y}{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-y/2}^{y/2}(y+1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(y+1)[x]_{-y/2}^{y/2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(y+1)[\\frac{y}{2}-(-\\frac{y}{2})]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2}+y)\\,dy \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{4}{2})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127325" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f1c4f276d3f3f1f755a0d39202b7b6efa6ee744e55963187e190c2f2a6a4a368"} {"question" "q-127325" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127325" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4"] ["math" "-\\frac{11}{2}"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{14}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127325" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{14}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127309" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨6y{x}^{2}+{e}^{y},\\,3{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y=\\sqrt{x}$ and the lines $y=0$ and $x=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127309" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/f6e80e3010ec7c40d3f59dbb1da4e77c985c889a720b6f954177d8ad83a01351.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=6y{x}^{2}+{e}^{y}$ and $Q(x,y)=3{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=12yx,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =6y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(12yx+6y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}(12yx+6y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(6{y}^{2}x+3{y}^{2})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(6{x}^{2}+3x)\\,dx \\\\  & =(2{x}^{3}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2})\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =22-0 \\\\  & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127309" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f1c4f276d3f3f1f755a0d39202b7b6efa6ee744e55963187e190c2f2a6a4a368"} {"question" "q-127309" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127309" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "17"] ["math" "22"] ["math" "25"] ["math" "\\frac{45}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{50}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127309" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "22"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127365" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨30xy,\\,15{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $x=y,$ $\\,x=-\\sqrt{y},$ and the line $y=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127365" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/24369439bc62cf06084a43d97dff0cee4e4089fd103511ef73d9dd5af1256ebb.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=30xy$ and $Q(x,y)=15{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=30y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =30y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):\\sqrt{y}\\le x\\le y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(30y+30y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{y}}^{y}60y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =60{\\int }_{0}^{1}y[x]_{-\\sqrt{y}}^{y}\\,dy \\\\  & =60{\\int }_{0}^{1}y(y+\\sqrt{y})\\,dy \\\\  & =60{\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}+{y}^{3/2})\\,dy \\\\  & =60{[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{2{y}^{5/2}}{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =60(\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{2}{5})-0 \\\\  & =44.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127365" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f1c4f276d3f3f1f755a0d39202b7b6efa6ee744e55963187e190c2f2a6a4a368"} {"question" "q-127365" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127365" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-50"] ["math" "-\\frac{55}{4}"] ["math" "37"] ["math" "44"] ["math" "\\frac{35}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "q-127365" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "44"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6ae8e438ffad33c9bbf5a7719e2b610289e972b960e64eaf05578a4177c82ea"} {"question" "e-9564" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-1,0), (-1,-3), (1,-3),$ and $(1,0),$  traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = (7x^2y^6 +x^3)\\, \\mathbf{i} + (- 2xy^7+4x^2y) \\, \\mathbf{j}$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/example-9564.json" "file_sha256" "cce8b7a5496009656b306ec6cabb963ab797f8069e5b5011f8046b81b469ff98"} {"question" "e-9564" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s = \\iint\\limits_D \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F\\,\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\,\\mathrm{d}y - Q \\, \\mathrm{d}x = \\iint\\limits_D \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\right) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/02964b07e45daba2d560fa16d34bcc92ddb8d0e0e83f644f8284dbe9d4b4557b.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y) =  7x^2y^6 +x^3$ and $Q(x,y) =- 2xy^7+4x^2y.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=14x{y}^{6}+3{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-14x{y}^{6}+4{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\{(x,y) :  -1\\leq x\\leq 1, \\, -3\\leq y \\leq 0\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(14x{y}^{6}+3{x}^{2})+(-14x{y}^{6}+4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-3}^{0}7{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/example-9564.json" "file_sha256" "cce8b7a5496009656b306ec6cabb963ab797f8069e5b5011f8046b81b469ff98"} {"question" "e-9565" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle \\sin(\\pi y) - x , \\: \\cos(\\pi x) + 2xy \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square with vertices $(0,-1), (2,-1), (2,1),$ and $(0,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/example-9565.json" "file_sha256" "1992ab41fcfcdabbf9dda96dfb7a2f7c6a2fb08928a47dc17cdd8b2bd0119d83"} {"question" "e-9565" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s = \\iint\\limits_D \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F\\,\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\,\\mathrm{d}y - Q \\, \\mathrm{d}x = \\iint\\limits_D \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\right) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/2f3374de4a29c62587a79f6286dfe788e64c37e09e9d9b83b3a67b4dac10dc21.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y) = \\sin(\\pi y) - x$ and $Q(x,y) = \\cos(\\pi x) + 2xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) :  0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\, -1 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1+2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x-1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}-x)\\,{|}_{0}^{2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({2}^{2}-2)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\,dy \\\\  & =2y\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2(1)-2(-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/example-9565.json" "file_sha256" "1992ab41fcfcdabbf9dda96dfb7a2f7c6a2fb08928a47dc17cdd8b2bd0119d83"} {"question" "e-9563" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle -3xy^2 , \\: y+\\cos x \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the triangle with vertices $(0,0), (2,2),$ and $(-2,2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/example-9563.json" "file_sha256" "1e62be0a6f11cbf91f0bb0d0f108022f7fa3162eb587f41a454801f504416712"} {"question" "e-9563" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s = \\iint\\limits_D \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F\\,\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\,\\mathrm{d}y - Q \\, \\mathrm{d}x = \\iint\\limits_D \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\right) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/assets/aa822f0b9259d6d9eb5c6f439588e70d4b9bee16b2b868fae36c7cbac72d3c3f.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y) = -3xy^2$ and $Q(x,y) = y+\\cos x .$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) :  -y \\leq x \\leq y, \\, 0 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-3{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-y}^{y}(-3{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-3{y}^{2}+1)[x]_{-y}^{y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-3{y}^{2}+1)(y-(-y))\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-3{y}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2y\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-6{y}^{3}+2y)\\,dy \\\\  & =[-6\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}+2\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{3{y}^{4}}{2}+{y}^{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(-24+4)-0 \\\\  & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062447/example-9563.json" "file_sha256" "1e62be0a6f11cbf91f0bb0d0f108022f7fa3162eb587f41a454801f504416712"}] "usage" {"q-127072" [] "e-9563" [] "q-126820" [] "e-9565" [] "e-9564" []} "basis" 1997 "retractions" [[17592186172241 :question/problem "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-1, 0), (2, 0), (2, 2)$, and $(-1, 2)$, traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n\n$F(x, y) = x^{2}yi + 5xyj$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral."] [17592186172238 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$F(x, y) = ⟨e^{y} + 2x^{2}y, e^{x} + 2xy^{2}⟩$\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(-1, 0), (2, 0), (2, 1)$, and $(-1, 1)$."] [17592186171838 :question/problem "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-1,0), (-1,-3), (1,-3)$, and $(1,0)$, traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = (7x^2y^6 +x^3)\\, \\mathbf{i} + (-2xy^7+4x^2y) \\, \\mathbf{j}$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral."] [17592186171838 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C$. If\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nand $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s = \\iint\\limits_D \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F\\,\\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\,\\mathrm{d}y - Q \\, \\mathrm{d}x = \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\right) \\,\\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3361/Source/Images/558dcb377eb7f6945abd065d2bcdb9e0.png)\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = 7x^2y^6 +x^3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x,y) =-2xy^7+4x^2y\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x} = 14xy^{6} + 3x^{2}, \\frac{∂Q}{∂y} &= -14xy^{6} + 4x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\{(x,y): -1\\leq x\\leq 1, \\, -3\\leq y \\leq 0\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)Pdy - Qdx &= ∬_(D)(\\frac{∂P}{∂x} + \\frac{∂Q}{∂y})dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(14xy^{6} + 3x^{2}) + (-14xy^{6} + 4x^{2})dA \\\\\n&= ∫_{-1}^{1}∫_{-3}^{0}7x^{2}dydx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186171839 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle \\sin(\\pi y) - x, \\: \\cos(\\pi x) + 2xy \\right\\rangle$\nthrough the boundary of the square with vertices $(0,-1), (2,-1), (2,1)$, and $(0,1)$."] [17592186171839 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C$. If\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nand $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s = \\iint\\limits_D \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F\\,\\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\,\\mathrm{d}y - Q \\, \\mathrm{d}x = \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\right) \\,\\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3361/Source/Images/16b1ce554d0410d587294f9657e5e669.png)\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = \\sin(\\pi y) - x\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x,y) = \\cos(\\pi x) + 2xy\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x} =-1, \\frac{∂Q}{∂y} &= 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y): 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\, -1 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)F \\cdot nds &= ∮_(C)Pdy - Qdx \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(\\frac{∂P}{∂x} + \\frac{∂Q}{∂y})dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(-1 + 2x)dA \\\\\n&= ∫_{-1}^{1}∫_{0}^{2}(2x - 1)dxdy \\\\\n&= ∫_{-1}^{1}(x^{2} - x)\\begin{vmatrix}_{0}^{2}dy \\\\ = ∫_{-1}^{1}(2^{2} - 2)dy \\\\ = ∫_{-1}^{1}2dy \\\\ = 2y\\end{vmatrix}_{-1}^{1} \\\\\n&= 2(1) - 2(-1) \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186171837 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle -3xy^2, \\: y+\\cos x \\right\\rangle$\nthrough the boundary of the triangle with vertices $(0,0), (2,2)$, and $(-2,2)$."] [17592186171837 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C$. If\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nand $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s = \\iint\\limits_D \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F\\,\\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\,\\mathrm{d}y - Q \\, \\mathrm{d}x = \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\right) \\,\\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3361/Source/Images/795c7197b03931fd6387f1c32465d057.png)\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = -3xy^2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x,y) = y+\\cos x\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x} =-3y^{2}, \\frac{∂Q}{∂y} &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y): -y \\leq x \\leq y, \\, 0 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)F \\cdot nds &= ∮_(C)Pdy - Qdx \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(\\frac{∂P}{∂x} + \\frac{∂Q}{∂y})dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(-3y^{2} + 1)dA \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}∫_{-y}^{y}(-3y^{2} + 1)dxdy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}(-3y^{2} + 1)[x]_{-y}^{y}dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}(-3y^{2} + 1)(y - (-y))dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}(-3y^{2} + 1) \\cdot 2ydy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}(-6y^{3} + 2y)dy \\\\\n&= [- 6 \\cdot \\frac{y^{4}}{4} + 2 \\cdot \\frac{y^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= [- \\frac{3y^{4}}{2} + y^{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= (-24 + 4) - 0 \\\\\n&=-20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186168632 17592186168633 17592186168634 17592186168635 17592186168636 17592186168650 17592186168651 17592186168652 17592186168653 17592186168654] "immutable_fields" [[17592186168631 :answer-field/correct] [17592186168631 :answer-field/id] [17592186168631 :answer-field/key] [17592186168631 :answer-field/type] [17592186168649 :answer-field/correct] [17592186168649 :answer-field/id] [17592186168649 :answer-field/key] [17592186168649 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
